Apple last week announced the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a> and &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max, arriving two years after the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-16/">iPhone 16</a> Pro and &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max. Skipping a generation means makes for a bigger upgrade, with more than 50 differences between the 2024 and 2026 models.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/iPhone-16-Pro-vs-18-Pro-Buyers-Guide-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1065251" /><br/>
Most of the visible change arrived in between, with the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17-pro/">iPhone 17 Pro</a> replacing the titanium frame and glass back with an aluminum unibody and Ceramic Shield 2, adding vapor chamber cooling, and upgrading both the telephoto and front cameras. This year's update is largely focused on internal changes, centering on the A20 Pro chip, a variable-aperture Main camera, a smaller <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/dynamic-island/">Dynamic Island</a>, and a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/iphone-18-pro-max-battery-capacities/">battery capacity increase</a> that delivers the largest jump in battery life in the iPhone's history. For &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro owners, the two sets of changes stack on top of each other.<br/>
<br/>
