<h2 id="which_to_choose">Which to Choose?</h2><br/>
For &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro and &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max owners, the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; is a worthwhile upgrade, and a far easier recommendation than it is for anyone <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/iphone-17-pro-vs-18-pro/">coming from last year's model</a>. Two generations of changes accumulate, touching almost every part of the device: an all-new aluminum unibody with Ceramic Shield 2 and three times better scratch resistance, a display that is 50% brighter outdoors at 3,000 nits and features an anti-reflective coating the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro lacks entirely, and a battery that runs for 36 hours rather than 27, rising from 33 hours to 45 hours on the Pro Max. Charging has changed most dramatically of all, dropping from 50% in 30 minutes to 50% in around 15 minutes.<br/>
<br/>
The camera differences are also major. The telephoto camera moves from 12 megapixels at 5x to 48 megapixels with 4x and 8x optical options, the front camera jumps from 12 megapixels to 18 megapixels and gains Center Stage and ultra-stabilized video, and the Main camera trades its fixed ƒ/1.78 aperture for a four-stop variable aperture ranging from ƒ/1.48 to ƒ/4.0. Add ProRes RAW, Genlock, Dual Capture, Cinematic mode at 60 fps, and manual Pro controls, and the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; is a substantially different camera system.<br/>
<br/>
Performance is the other argument for upgrading, particularly if you have noticed your &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro throttling. The A20 Pro moves to a 2nm process, gains a seventh GPU core with Neural Accelerators, and doubles the Neural Engine to 32 cores, while the vapor chamber that the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro lacks entirely is now in its second generation. If you play demanding games, shoot long video clips, or expect to lean on on-device <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-intelligence/">Apple Intelligence</a> features for several more years, the extra 4GB of RAM and the doubled Neural Engine make the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; the more future-proof purchase.<br/>
<br/>
That being said, the price has moved against you. The &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; costs &#36;200 more than the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro did at launch, even accounting for the doubled 256GB entry tier, and the &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max is &#36;100 more than its equivalent. The &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj; Max is also 22 grams heavier than the &zwnj;iPhone 16&zwnj; Pro Max, which is noticeable in the hand, although it buys you the largest battery gain Apple has ever delivered on an iPhone and the option of a 2TB device.<br/>
<br/>
A notable exception arises if you are upgrading primarily for the design, the telephoto camera, or the battery. Almost all of those enhancements arrived with the &zwnj;iPhone 17 Pro&zwnj; rather than this year's model, so an &zwnj;iPhone 17 Pro&zwnj; bought from a third-party retailer at a heavy discount will get you most of the way there for meaningfully less money. The &zwnj;iPhone 18 Pro&zwnj;'s genuine exclusives over that model are the variable aperture and Pro controls, the C2 modem, the smaller &zwnj;Dynamic Island&zwnj;, and roughly three extra hours of battery life. If none of those matter to you, last year's model is the better value.<br/>
